    "result_text": "The full test suite passes. `AcceptanceTest` is the only executable test class, and it's now green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in `TelephoneNormalizer.toE164`, which is the class that owns telephone parsing/normalization. The controller (`OwnerRestControllerV1`) already returns **400 Bad Request** whenever `toE164` yields `Optional.empty()`, so no controller changes were needed.\n\n**Change** (`TelephoneNormalizer.java`):\n- Added a prefix-free lookup table `NATIONAL_DIGITS_BY_COUNTRY_CODE = {\"1\": 10, \"61\": 9}` mapping each recognised E.164 calling code to its required national-number digit count.\n- Added a small single-responsibility helper `hasValidNationalLength(digits)` that finds the matching country code prefix and checks that `digits.length() - countryCode.length()` equals the required national length. Unrecognised country codes fall back to the existing generic `MIN_DIGITS`/`MAX_DIGITS` bounds only.\n- Wired it into `toE164` after the generic length check, so a wrong national length yields `Optional.empty()` \u2192 400.\n\nThis correctly rejects `+61 123` (2 national digits, needs 9) and, for example, a `+1` number without exactly 10 national digits, while the valid AU mobile fixtures (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 `+61` + 9 national digits) and any `+1` NANP numbers with 10 national digits still pass.\n\nBecause E.164 country codes form a prefix-free set, at most one entry matches, so the lookup order is unambiguous. All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
